# Upper Bound for the Multicolor Ramsey Number $r(\underbrace{3,\dots,3}_{n})$

Let $r_n := r(\underbrace{3,\dots,3}_{n})$ denote the $n$-color Ramsey number in which every color is required to avoid a monochromatic triangle. We prove the classical bound

$$\boxed{\,r_n \;\le\; \lfloor e\cdot n!\rfloor + 1 \;<\; e\cdot n! + 1\, .}$$

---

## Lemma (multicolor Ramsey recurrence)

For $r\ge 2$ and integers $k_1,\dots,k_r\ge 2$,

$$R(k_1,\dots,k_r)\;\le\;\sum_{i=1}^{r} R(k_1,\dots,k_i-1,\dots,k_r)\;-\;(r-2). \tag{$\star$}$$

*Proof.* Set $R_i:=R(k_1,\dots,k_i-1,\dots,k_r)$ and $N:=\sum_i R_i-(r-2)$. In any $r$-coloring of the edges of $K_N$, fix a vertex $v$ and let $C_i$ be the set of neighbors joined to $v$ by an edge of color $i$. Then

$$|C_1|+\cdots+|C_r|=N-1=\sum_{i}(R_i-1)+1.$$

If $|C_i|\le R_i-1$ for every $i$, the total would be at most $\sum_i(R_i-1)=N-2<N-1$, a contradiction. Hence $|C_i|\ge R_i$ for some $i$. The induced coloring on $C_i$ (on $\ge R_i$ vertices) contains, by definition of $R_i$, either a color-$j$ copy of $K_{k_j}$ for some $j\ne i$, or a color-$i$ copy of $K_{k_i-1}$. In the latter case, adjoining $v$ — which is color-$i$-adjacent to every vertex of $C_i$ — yields a color-$i$ copy of $K_{k_i}$. In either case a monochromatic $K_{k_j}$ arises. $\square$

---

## Applying the recurrence

Recall the reduction identity $R(2,k_2,\dots,k_r)=R(k_2,\dots,k_r)$: a color forced to avoid even a single edge must simply not be used, collapsing to $r-1$ colors. Therefore

$$R(\underbrace{3,\dots,2,\dots,3}_{n\text{ entries}})=R(\underbrace{3,\dots,3}_{n-1})=r_{n-1},$$

and every one of the $n$ terms on the right-hand side of $(\star)$ equals $r_{n-1}$. With $r=n$ this gives

$$r_n\;\le\; n\,r_{n-1}-(n-2). \tag{1}$$

Set $s_n:=r_n-1$. Then (1) becomes

$$s_n\;\le\; n\,s_{n-1}+1,\qquad s_1=r_1-1=R(3)-1=3-1=2. \tag{2}$$

## Solving the recurrence

Divide (2) by $n!$ and write $t_n:=s_n/n!$:

$$t_n\;\le\; t_{n-1}+\frac{1}{n!},\qquad t_1=\frac{2}{1!}=2.$$

Telescoping,

$$t_n\;\le\;2+\sum_{j=2}^{n}\frac{1}{j!}\;=\;\sum_{j=0}^{n}\frac{1}{j!}.$$

Multiplying back by $n!$,

$$s_n\;\le\;\sum_{j=0}^{n}\frac{n!}{j!}\;=:\;A_n. \tag{3}$$

Each summand $n!/j!$ is an integer, so $A_n$ is a positive integer.

## Relating $A_n$ to $e\cdot n!$

Since $e=\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}1/j!$,

$$e\cdot n!=\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{n!}{j!}=A_n+\underbrace{\sum_{j=n+1}^{\infty}\frac{n!}{j!}}_{\text{tail}}.$$

For $n\ge 1$ the tail satisfies

$$0\;<\;\text{tail}\;=\;\frac{1}{n+1}+\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}+\cdots\;\le\;\frac{1}{n+1}\Bigl(1+\tfrac{1}{n+1}+\tfrac{1}{(n+1)^2}+\cdots\Bigr)=\frac{1}{n}\le 1,$$

and in fact $\text{tail}<1$ for $n\ge 2$ (and $=1$ only in the degenerate limit). Thus $0<e\cdot n!-A_n<1$, which is exactly

$$A_n=\lfloor e\cdot n!\rfloor. \tag{4}$$

## Conclusion

Combining (3) and (4),

$$r_n-1=s_n\le A_n=\lfloor e\cdot n!\rfloor,$$

and therefore

$$\boxed{\,r(\underbrace{3,\dots,3}_{n})\;=\;r_n\;\le\;\lfloor e\cdot n!\rfloor+1\;<\;e\cdot n!+1.\,}$$

*Sanity checks.* For $n=1$: $\lfloor e\rfloor+1=2+1=3=R(3)$. For $n=2$: $\lfloor 2e\rfloor+